;;; A premise whose relation is a parameter its activator binds to `eq` scans
;;; stored facts — regression `relvar-eq-premise`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`), under Q-M1e2.26: the reading holds wherever
;;; a relation can be written.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts `(p a)` and `(p b)`, and a rule whose third premise's relation
;;; is its parameter `?R`, activated by `(r eq)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Under `(r eq)` the third premise is `(eq ?x ?y)`, the relation of
;;; equality, whose facts are computed: the rule fires on `(a, a)` and `(b,
;;; b)`, so root holds `(hit a a)` and `(hit b b)`. H is `(hit a b)` and `(hit
;;; b a)`, which no rule refutes, so R is four states, each holding root's two.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `dc4c0bdc`, release and debug alike: the premise was a pattern over
;;; stored `eq` facts, which no state holds, so the rule never fired, root
;;; held no `hit`, and R was four states, none holding `(hit a a)` or `(hit b
;;; b)`, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE`, `ein oracle FILE`).

(relation p Node :why "{?3} is a node")
(relation hit Node Node :why "{?3} and {?4} are two nodes")
(p a)
(p b)
(rule r (?R)
  :match (and (p ?x) (p ?y) (?R ?x ?y))
  :assert (hit ?x ?y)
  :why "{?x} and {?y} are nodes in the relation {?R}")
(r eq)
